## Resumable automation actions

Read `143-tools capability list` for the configured `automation_actions` kinds and destinations. Use only actions needed for the goal. Select a stable operation_key for the logical workflow (for example daily-report:2026-09-22) and an action_key for each step. Reuse these keys after interruptions and across runs; use new operation keys for genuinely distinct occurrences. A successful replay returns reused: true and the original receipt timestamps; it did not send again.

First inspect `143-tools automation action-status --operation_key KEY`. To execute a new step, write a JSON file containing operation_key, action_key, kind, and its content, then call `143-tools automation execute-action --file PATH`.
- github_label and github_team_review use their configured label/team; github_issue_comment also takes text. All GitHub actions require pr_number and the current full head_sha.
- When explicitly configured, github_issue_comment_update replaces the body of this automation's existing PR comment. Supply text and source_action_id, the UUID of the original successful github_issue_comment receipt. Read that ID from the original receipt or the exact server marker `<!-- 143-automation-action:UUID -->` in the prior comment body. The server checks the receipt, scope, ownership, and expected body. Omit the reserved 143 server markers from text; preserve any application-specific markers required by the goal. Keep the original source_action_id across revisions, give each intentional revision a distinct action key within its operation, and reuse the same keys and frozen content on retries. If the update is blocked, report it without creating a replacement comment.
- When explicitly configured, github_pull_request_review submits an advisory GitHub COMMENT review. Supply one to five comments with path, line, and body on the right side of the current PR diff, plus optional text for the review summary. The server mentions the verified PR author and records the review receipt. Omit mentions, reserved 143 markers, and duplicate findings.
- slack_notification takes text. The server adds an optional admin-configured Slack user group mention. A link shaped like [PR #N](https://github.com/OWNER/REPO/pull/N#pullrequestreview-ID) renders with a clickable PR label when the repository matches this session and both PR numbers match. Keep mention markup out of text. notion_tracking_row takes properties, a map from configured property names to string values; supported types are title, rich_text, url, date (YYYY-MM-DD), and select (an existing option).
- Slack and Notion may omit PR preconditions in every run mode. Include pr_number and head_sha when the content is valid only for that commit; an explicit precondition is always enforced, including on resume. In a continuous target session any supplied PR must match that target.
- To resume a recorded pending or definitively failed step, use `143-tools automation execute-action --resume --operation_key KEY --action_key KEY`. This uses frozen content. Changed content under the same keys is rejected.
- A succeeded action already has its receipt: do not repeat it under another key. An in_progress action has an active sender. For an unknown github_pull_request_review, --resume with its original keys searches GitHub for the existing review without sending again. If the action stays unknown, report its ID for operator reconciliation. Never retry an uncertain send using direct provider APIs or fresh keys.

After any interrupted command, inspect status before proceeding. Report actual receipts and incomplete or uncertain steps. Status covers recorded steps only; it does not prove the whole goal is complete. Execute each step independently and preserve any ordering required by the goal.
